body:
I can confirm that in almost all cases we don't add more elements after a call to reserve, so it might make sense to make reserve always tight and only grow when the capacity is reached by a push_back call.
But in order to get this PR moving, I agree with @Ivorforce and would actually not do this for now. Instead, I'd go for:
```cpp
void reserve(U p_size) {
	if (p_size <= capacity) {
		return;
	}

	if (tight) {
		capacity = p_size;
	} else {
		while (p_size > capacity) {
			capacity = MAX((U)2, capacity + ((1 + capacity) >> 1));
		}
	}
	data = (T *)memrealloc(data, capacity * sizeof(T));
	CRASH_COND_MSG(!data, "Out of memory");
}
```

